<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Multan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Multan is an ancient city in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> captured it in 1818, extending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>. A revolt at Multan in 1848 sparked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-sikh-war">Second Anglo-Sikh War</a>, which ended with the British annexation of the Punjab.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/multan.jpg" alt="Multan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yaarahmadd · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shrine_Shah_Rukn-e-Alam.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Multan lies on the eastern bank of the Chenab River in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. The region holds archaeological sites of the early Harappan period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>, dated between 3000 and 2800 BCE. Hindu texts say the sage Kashyapa founded it and that it was the capital of the Trigarta Kingdom during the Kurukshetra War of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>. In the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> the city stood on an island in the Ravi River, which has since shifted course many times. The origin of its name is unclear; an ancient name was Malli-istan, after the Malli tribe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Multan is believed to have been the Malli capital that Alexander besieged in 326 BCE. During the siege of the citadel Alexander was wounded by an arrow that penetrated his lung. A Muslim army first invaded after the reign of the caliph Ali, in 664, but the expedition seems to have been one of exploration. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-qasim">Muhammad ibn Qasim</a> took the city in 712 after a two-month siege, and its subjects remained mostly non-Muslim for some decades under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-munabbih">Banu Munabbih</a> came to rule Multan by the mid-800s, and in 855 it became capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Multan. Multan attracted many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> mystics in the 11th and 12th centuries and is called the City of Saints. In 1445 it became capital of the Langah Sultanate. Akbar made it the headquarters of the Multan Subah, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>'s largest provinces, and Mughal rule was re-established there in 1557.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> captured Multan in 1752, and after repeated invasions following the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> the city declined from an important early-modern commercial centre to a regional trading town. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> ruled it from 1818 to 1849. By December 1848 the British had taken parts of the outskirts and destroyed the Multan Fort in bombardment; on 22 January 1849 they breached its walls and Diwan Mulraj surrendered. Between the 1890s and 1920s the British laid a vast network of canals across the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Multan is the administrative headquarters of its division and district, and a major cultural, religious and economic centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. With nearby Uch it is known for its large number of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> shrines, many dating from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. The old city, like other ancient cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, had a walled town and a royal citadel, but Multan lost its citadel, largely destroyed by the British in 1848.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Multan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was contested by rival Ghurid successors such as Taj al-Din Yildiz of Ghazni and Nasir al-Din Qabacha of <b>Multan</b> and by resistant Rajput rulers, and its administration rested on personal loyalty, not on institutions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Marathas under Raghunathrao drove Timur Shah from Lahore, <b>Multan</b> and Kashmir in 1757, Ahmad declared a jihad, gathering warriors of many Afghan tribes and the Baloch under Mir Nasir Khan I, Khan of Kalat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty were Sunnis of the Hanafi school who presented themselves as champions of the Abbasid caliphate, persecuting Ismailis in <b>Multan</b> and Shi'is and Mu'tazilites at Rayy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>He captured <b>Multan</b> in 1175 but was defeated by the Chaulukyas of Gujarat at Kasahrada in 1178, and he ended Ghaznavid rule in the Punjab by taking Lahore in 1186.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>On the western frontier the empire held the Arab principalities of Sindh and <b>Multan</b> in check; Arab writers report that the rulers of Multan threatened to destroy its famous sun-temple idol whenever a Pratihara army approached.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>They halted expansion to the east but spared the empire a direct confrontation with the British, and Ranjit Singh turned his armies north and west towards <b>Multan</b>, Kashmir and the Khyber frontier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
